title: Simplified query language
tags: Query
kind: overview
aliases: simplified-query simplified [[simplified query]] grammar query-grammar
summary: A short, forgiving query surface whose whole syntax is an editable grammar; it expands on the client into the normal DSL.

The DSL is precise but wordy for everyday filtering (`genre = "jazz" AND rating IS
PRESENT`). The ''simplified'' language is shorter — `genre=jazz rating?` — and it is
the default input of the GUI's query box and of `-q … -s` on the command line.

It is not a second query engine: it ''expands into normal DSL text'', which is then
parsed and run as usual.

```
simplified text → lexer → grammar → DSL text → DSL parser → query → daemon
```

Nothing in it is built in. Its entire syntax — the boolean skeleton, every
predicate form, the tag and date macros, the field aliases — is a grammar file you
can edit (`~/.config/metafolder/core/query-grammar`), each rule carrying the DSL text
it produces. The shipped rules are defaults, not features.

So the simplified language is ''not'' a superset of the DSL: a grammar may accept a
DSL query, or not, or something else entirely, and expanding DSL text is not a no-op.
The two stay explicit — the GUI shows both, the CLI takes simplified text only with
`-s` — and a DSL form has a simplified spelling only if a grammar rule gives it one.

